 and pencils?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"65505bc9361a1f7501f250eecefd9e970e40a4cd9dc7cdb0cc04a28a156c06e9"},"seconds":9.434180498123169,"seed":822821469097985174,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the sets as follows:\n- Let \\( P \\) be the set of boxes that contain pencils.\n- Let \\( Q \\) be the set of boxes that contain pens.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- The total number of boxes is 20.\n- The number of boxes that contain pencils is 13, so \\( |P| = 13 \\).\n- The number of boxes that contain pens is 9, so \\( |Q| = 9 \\).\n- The number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils is 3.\n\nFirst, we need to find the number of boxes that contain either pencils, pens, or both. Since there are 3 boxes that contain neither, the number of boxes that contain either pencils, pens, or both is:\n\\[ 20 - 3 = 17. \\]\n\nThis can be expressed using the principle of inclusion and exclusion as:\n\\[ |P \\cup Q| = |P| + |Q| - |P \\cap Q|. \\]\nWe know that \\( |P \\cup Q| = 17 \\), \\( |P| = 13 \\), and \\( |Q| = 9 \\). Plugging these values into the equation, we get:\n\\[ 17 = 13 + 9 - |P \\cap Q|. \\]\n\nTo find \\( |P \\cap Q| \\), we solve the equation:\n\\[ 17 = 22 - |P \\cap Q|. \\]\n\\[ |P \\cap Q| = 22 - 17. \\]\n\\[ |P \\cap Q| = 5. \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of boxes that contain both pens and pencils is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:101"}
